president Trump applauded for its new  1.2 billion dollar investment at three  Michigan plants he tweeted yesterday car  companies coming back to the u.s. jobs  jobs jobs exclamation point last night  on the CBS Evening News Jean Reynolds  reported that Ford's investment will  only bring about 130 new production jobs  the president's tweet was the latest  example of mr. Trump touting u.s. job  creation under his watch job supporing  back you saw what happened with Exxon  where they just announced a massive jobs  program  Charter Communications has just  committed to investing 25 billion  dollars and it's committed further to  hiring 20,000 American workers over the  next four years since my election Ford  Fiat Chrysler General Motors Sprint  Softbank Lockheed Intel Walmart and many  others have announced that they will  invest billions and billions of dollars  in the United States and will create  tens of thousands of new American jobs  Gerald sybiz Executive Washington  editor-in-chief commentator for The Wall  Street Journal and joins us from  Washington this morning Gerry good to  see you thank you  does the president deserve the credit he  appears to be taking for the jobs jobs  jobs well he deserves some credit but I  mean this is a classic case in in the  Trump world in which more credit is  being claimed that's probably do look  companies make no secret about the fact  that they think a deregulatory  environment which the Trump  administration is going to create and  the prospect of a big tax cut is  encouraging them to invest some more  money but the White House is taking  credit for jobs that were going to be  created anyway the Charter  Communications jobs were committed to in  2015 as part of a merger the four jobs  that the White House is taking credit  for were committed to by and large as  part of a 2015 deal with United Auto  Workers so what's happening right now is  I think that a lot of companies are  framing decisions they were going to  make anyway or had or had already made  as a response to the Trump  administration's arrival and the White  House is gobbling that up and taking  credit for it so there's some truth in  there but not as much as you're led to  believe Jerry there was a story in The  New York Times today about Blackrock  huge investment and managed  company that they are now going to use  robots rather than asset managers to  manage portfolios and that therefore  they were laying off some employees how  big of impact is technology gonna have  on job creation well you know I think  that's the bigger story within this  story you know there's been a lot of  debate about how much job loss there  comes comes from trade how much job loss  comes from unfair competition abroad but  the real job killer in America in a lot  of ways particularly manufacturing  automation you know robot comes into a  factory workers go away and that's  happening across the the manufacturing  sector and as that Black Rock story  indicates is now seeping into other  sectors as well and so I think that  automation is in its effect on  employment is probably the real story  here it just doesn't get as much  attention because the political debate  is much more about for example trade but  I think increasingly we're going to be  seeing questions about what a workers do  when automation is the problem not when  Mexico or China is the problem it's good  to hear the president talking up jobs  any president wants to do that and  Americans respond to that the question  is whether he can deliver on those  promises yesterday of course in  announcing rolling back some of those  energy regulations he talked about  bringing putting coal miners back to  work but there's a the the founder and  executive of Murray energy which is the  largest privately held coal miner in the  u.s. said that he told Trump directly I  suggested he temper his expectations he  can't bring them back yeah well I happen  to know some some lawmakers Republican  lawmakers from coal states are telling  him the same thing you know pull back  the the rhetoric a little bit because  you have to temper the promises you know  it is true that the the coal action will  give a reprieve to some coal-fired power  plants and I talked to some people down  in Southwest Virginia a couple of weeks  ago who said there are some people in  the cold industry being called back into  work now as a result of all this but the  long-term trends are still the long-term  trends natural gas supplies are going up  because of fracking the cost of  renewable energy sources is going down  as the technology improves and so  industry is moving on a long-term path  away from coal that's not going to stop  again there's going to be some  short-term beneficial impact in the coal  industry but not a change in this  long-term  and that's what the Whitehouse probably  needs to keep in mind as it engages in  this rhetoric about saving coal jobs you  know the president has been known to use  this Twitter account when he hears about  companies who are US companies who are  saying they're going to take jobs out of  the country do you think that affects  some decisions that companies are  planning to make or do make you know I  think it probably does I do think  executives sit in the c-suite now and  ask themselves do we want to be put in a  position in which we're attacked by the  White House for a decision that we might  make that's in the margins on the other  hand if we're gonna make a decision that  the White House likes do we not want to  get some credit for that that's a that's  a good thing for our for our company so  I do think it's probably affecting  decisions at least in the margins but  again you have to distinguish that from  long term trends and things that we're  going to happen anyway  all right Jerry Saad thank you always  good to have you here thanks a lot  appreciate it
 

